/* The integer twin of the real-valued rotation beside this file: the same
   problem, the same property, and the only difference is the sort.  This one
   answers valid in under a second with the shipped template, where the real one
   does not answer at all -- which is what makes the pair worth keeping.  Over
   the integers a linear ranking function decreases by a whole unit whenever it
   decreases, which is what the templates' decrease condition asks for; over the
   reals the state can be scaled arbitrarily close to the origin, so no linear
   form in (a,b) is guaranteed to drop by one and the rank has to be piecewise
   constant instead. */
Main 0
s.t.
Main (z:int): bool =mu forall (a:int). forall (b:int). (a = 0 /\ b = 0) \/ Q a b;
Q (a:int) (b:int): bool =mu a > 0 /\ b >= 0 \/ Q (-b) a;
